WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.567
-Our next guest
is an Emmy-nominated actor

00:00:02.567 --> 00:00:04.634
you know from his work
in "Full Metal Jacket"

00:00:04.634 --> 00:00:06.200
and as the mysterious
Dr. Brenner

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.367
on "Stranger Things."

00:00:07.367 --> 00:00:10.501
The highly anticipated
volume 2 of season 4

00:00:10.501 --> 00:00:13.133
is available to stream
on Netflix starting July 1st.

00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:14.634
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.634 --> 00:00:19.267
-All I've ever wanted to do
is to help you.

00:00:19.267 --> 00:00:24.501
And right now, I think
you very much need my help.

00:00:24.501 --> 00:00:27.634
Your gifts have been stolen.

00:00:27.634 --> 00:00:30.467
I believe I know why.

00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:34.868
And I believe
I know how to get them back.

00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:39.934
Let us work together again,
you and I.

00:00:39.934 --> 00:00:42.801
Daughter...and Papa.

00:00:42.801 --> 00:00:45.767
-Please welcome to the show
Matthew Modine, everybody!

00:00:45.767 --> 00:00:47.267
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:57.267
♪♪

00:00:57.267 --> 00:00:59.567
♪♪

00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:02.167
Welcome!
I'm so happy you're here!

00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:03.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:03.634 --> 00:01:04.801
-I'm so happy to be here.

00:01:04.801 --> 00:01:07.200
-So we're neighbors in New York,
I have heard.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.968
I've never seen you
around the neighborhood,

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:11.801
but you have seen me.
-I have seen you, yeah.

00:01:11.801 --> 00:01:14.100
I've often wanted
to come up and say hello,

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.067
but you're busy talking.
-Yeah.

00:01:16.067 --> 00:01:17.801
[ Laughter ]
-And I found out today

00:01:17.801 --> 00:01:19.467
that you have three children.
-Yeah.

00:01:19.467 --> 00:01:21.334
-And so I realize it might be
the only time

00:01:21.334 --> 00:01:24.467
you get an opportunity to talk
is to yourself,

00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:26.334
out on the streets of New York.
-Yeah.

00:01:26.334 --> 00:01:28.434
I mostly wanted you
to say that on TV

00:01:28.434 --> 00:01:31.267
'cause it'll make my wife so
happy, because she always says,

00:01:31.267 --> 00:01:33.834
"You have to stop
talking to yourself.

00:01:33.834 --> 00:01:37.167
People are gonna think
you're an insane person."

00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:40.133
So you were --
I remember this episode,

00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:44.367
but you were here in this
building in 1988 hosting "SNL."

00:01:44.367 --> 00:01:47.100
You have -- I want to say,
you have great hair now.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:49.100
Makes sense, 'cause you had
great hair then.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:50.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:50.334 --> 00:01:53.000
Look at that.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.501
And a suit jacket
for a much larger man.

00:01:57.501 --> 00:02:00.100
[ Laughter ]
-I know.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:01.567
What was that?
The big shoulder pads.

00:02:01.567 --> 00:02:03.434
-I know.
It's really --

00:02:03.434 --> 00:02:06.767
Yeah, I feel very lucky to have
not been famous in 1988.

00:02:06.767 --> 00:02:08.100
[ Laughter ]

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:09.968
Did you enjoy that experience
of doing "SNL"?

00:02:09.968 --> 00:02:12.567
-It was amazing.
Actually, just as I was

00:02:12.567 --> 00:02:16.267
coming out here, it reminded me
of being in that little --

00:02:16.267 --> 00:02:18.467
when you walk out
onto "Saturday Night Live,"

00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:20.467
you kind of stand
in a little coffin.

00:02:20.467 --> 00:02:22.467
-It's tiny.
-And then the door opens,

00:02:22.467 --> 00:02:24.734
and there's the incredible band.

00:02:24.734 --> 00:02:27.901
And, you know, as a child,
you watch those things,

00:02:27.901 --> 00:02:30.033
and then, suddenly,
you find yourself

00:02:30.033 --> 00:02:32.300
a working actor
and being invited to host

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:33.634
"Saturday Night Live."

00:02:33.634 --> 00:02:36.234
And now, all of a sudden,
you're walking into something

00:02:36.234 --> 00:02:38.834
that you've seen
hundreds of times,

00:02:38.834 --> 00:02:42.167
and it's you walking out
into that environment.

00:02:42.167 --> 00:02:45.801
And it's --
it's -- it's bizarre.

00:02:45.801 --> 00:02:49.400
But just before they opened
the door and I went out,

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:51.567
Lorne Michaels
stuck his head in, and he said,

00:02:51.567 --> 00:02:54.601
"Hey, kid, have a good time,"
and he shut the door.

00:02:54.601 --> 00:02:56.467
And I thought,
"Yeah, that's what you do.

00:02:56.467 --> 00:02:58.934
Go out and have a good time."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:58.934 --> 00:03:01.033
-I want to ask about
"Stranger Things."

00:03:01.033 --> 00:03:03.300
You know, the first season,
when you were in it,

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:04.868
I feel like not many TV shows

00:03:04.868 --> 00:03:06.901
have had premiere seasons
like this.

00:03:06.901 --> 00:03:09.701
And it must have introduced you
to a much younger generation

00:03:09.701 --> 00:03:10.934
doing a show
like "Stranger Things."

00:03:10.934 --> 00:03:13.601
Did people approach you
more once you were in it?

00:03:13.601 --> 00:03:17.534
-When the show broke,
I was doing a television --

00:03:17.534 --> 00:03:21.601
I don't know what --
like, a miniseries in Italy.

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:24.100
And all the little Italian
children were coming up

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:26.901
and saying, "Papa, Papa, Papa!"
[ Laughter ]

00:03:26.901 --> 00:03:28.434
And I thought,
"That's really weird.

00:03:28.434 --> 00:03:30.167
Why are they calling me Papa?"

00:03:30.167 --> 00:03:34.167
And my children call me Papa.
So...

00:03:34.167 --> 00:03:37.300
And then,
two plus two equaled four,

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:40.000
and I realized that it was
because of the show.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.501
And then -- you know, let's say
"Full Metal Jacket"

00:03:44.501 --> 00:03:46.701
is the most successful film
of my career,

00:03:46.701 --> 00:03:48.501
which just had
its 30th anniversary.

00:03:48.501 --> 00:03:50.968
-An incredible film
that endures.

00:03:50.968 --> 00:03:53.834
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Yeah.

00:03:53.834 --> 00:03:58.834
And at that time, there's 164
territories in the world.

00:03:58.834 --> 00:04:01.968
Netflix is in 162 territories.

00:04:01.968 --> 00:04:05.133
They're not, I think,
in North Korea and China.

00:04:05.133 --> 00:04:10.734
And so to have a success today
in that kind of regard

00:04:10.734 --> 00:04:14.534
is beyond anything
that you could ever imagine.

00:04:14.534 --> 00:04:17.400
It's really weird and bizarre.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:20.000
And kudos to all the young kids
on the show

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.033
for keeping their feet
on the ground.

00:04:23.033 --> 00:04:25.267
-Yeah.
-I mean, we've grown up

00:04:25.267 --> 00:04:27.968
seeing what this industry
can do to children,

00:04:27.968 --> 00:04:30.400
how it chews them up
and digests them.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.601
And Millie Bobby Brown,
who I have most of my work with

00:04:34.601 --> 00:04:36.534
on the show --
all of my work with --

00:04:36.534 --> 00:04:40.200
is just the loveliest,
you know, kindest,

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:42.734
grounded person that you ever
met, and I love her.

00:04:42.734 --> 00:04:45.501
-It's one of the nice, like,
sort of side stories about

00:04:45.501 --> 00:04:47.734
the "Stranger Things" phenomenon
is watching all those kids

00:04:47.734 --> 00:04:49.367
grow up and realize
that they're all good people.

00:04:49.367 --> 00:04:50.968
-Yeah, she was 11 years old
when we met.

00:04:50.968 --> 00:04:53.901
-It's crazy.
Do you -- you --

00:04:53.901 --> 00:04:55.167
Now, obviously,
this was a big twist

00:04:55.167 --> 00:04:56.534
that your character came back.

00:04:56.534 --> 00:04:59.567
I think a lot of people assumed
Dr. Brenner was dead.

00:04:59.567 --> 00:05:02.200
-Was dead.
-Did you know from the beginning

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:04.868
that you would come back
for this season?

00:05:04.868 --> 00:05:07.033
-Well, the Duffers
always promised

00:05:07.033 --> 00:05:08.234
that I was gonna come back.

00:05:08.234 --> 00:05:10.934
They didn't know how,
and they kept structuring

00:05:10.934 --> 00:05:14.033
the show like, "He's gonna come
back in a big way in season 2."

00:05:14.033 --> 00:05:20.968
And then I came just as a memory
or a phenomenon of a chimera.

00:05:20.968 --> 00:05:23.701
And then season 3,
they just said,

00:05:23.701 --> 00:05:26.467
"We're gonna save you
for season 4." And so...

00:05:26.467 --> 00:05:28.734
-Did you believe them,
or did you start doubting it?

00:05:28.734 --> 00:05:31.834
-I started doubting it.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:31.834 --> 00:05:33.567
-Once you knew --
I mean, obviously,

00:05:33.567 --> 00:05:36.234
this is one of those shows
where, you know, spoilers,

00:05:36.234 --> 00:05:37.634
obviously, are forbidden.

00:05:37.634 --> 00:05:39.400
The audience
doesn't want them, either.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:40.767
Did you have to keep the secret?

00:05:40.767 --> 00:05:42.667
Was it a tough secret
for you to keep?

00:05:42.667 --> 00:05:45.434
-It was.
You know, the other thing

00:05:45.434 --> 00:05:49.534
that didn't have -- exist a few
years ago was social media.

00:05:49.534 --> 00:05:52.701
By the way, there's an emo --
emo-ji? Emoji?

00:05:52.701 --> 00:05:55.367
-Yeah, emo-ji.
-Emo-ji.

00:05:55.367 --> 00:05:58.033
An emo-ji.
-"Papa, Papa, emo-ji!"

00:05:58.033 --> 00:06:02.534
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:06:02.534 --> 00:06:05.300
-When I'm texting to people,
I often use one.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:08.868
And when I text, I'm gonna use
the Seth Meyers emoji.

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:12.133
-Emoji, yeah.
What's the Seth Meyers emoji?

00:06:12.133 --> 00:06:13.400
[ Laughter ]

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:15.501
Wait.
You think the shrug emoji?

00:06:15.501 --> 00:06:17.467
-Yeah.
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:06:17.467 --> 00:06:19.434
-I don't think that's right.

00:06:19.434 --> 00:06:21.167
I don't think
that's very fair at all.

00:06:21.167 --> 00:06:22.801
[ Laughter ]
-I think -- there it is.

00:06:22.801 --> 00:06:24.067
-All right, there it is.
I don't know.

00:06:24.067 --> 00:06:27.434
Does that --
-Yeah.

00:06:27.434 --> 00:06:33.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:35.067
We just need to change
the color of his hair.

00:06:35.067 --> 00:06:37.767
-Yeah, exactly.
So, hey, you know,

00:06:37.767 --> 00:06:40.934
we've mentioned your work with
Kubrick on "Full Metal Jacket."

00:06:40.934 --> 00:06:43.901
And, you know, obviously,
you're in your 20s.

00:06:43.901 --> 00:06:45.534
Stanley Kubrick
is already Stanley Kubrick

00:06:45.534 --> 00:06:47.000
when he's making that film.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:48.667
What was it like to meet him
for the first time?

00:06:48.667 --> 00:06:50.367
And I would imagine
just auditioning for him,

00:06:50.367 --> 00:06:52.334
in general,
must have been terrifying.

00:06:52.334 --> 00:06:55.734
-Well, I didn't audition.
He sent me --

00:06:55.734 --> 00:06:58.834
I was at a diner in Los Angeles,

00:06:58.834 --> 00:07:01.133
a very famous restaurant
called The Source,

00:07:01.133 --> 00:07:03.567
and it was made famous
because Woody Allen,

00:07:03.567 --> 00:07:05.434
when he went out
to find Annie Hall,

00:07:05.434 --> 00:07:08.467
Diane Keaton, in "Annie Hall,"
he didn't know how --

00:07:08.467 --> 00:07:09.968
He was a New Yorker
and didn't know how

00:07:09.968 --> 00:07:11.801
to drive that big Cadillac.

00:07:11.801 --> 00:07:13.968
And he thinks
he's put the car in reverse

00:07:13.968 --> 00:07:15.167
and drives into the restaurant.

00:07:15.167 --> 00:07:17.067
So I was sitting with somebody
I know you know,

00:07:17.067 --> 00:07:18.367
David Alan Grier...
-Yeah.

00:07:18.367 --> 00:07:21.000
-...wonderful comedic actor
and dramatic actor.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:23.901
And we had just won this prize
at the Venice Film Festival

00:07:23.901 --> 00:07:26.534
for Best Actor,
and we were hoping

00:07:26.534 --> 00:07:29.367
that Woody Allen was in New York
and not in Los Angeles

00:07:29.367 --> 00:07:30.934
driving around
looking for Annie Hall,

00:07:30.934 --> 00:07:33.634
because in movies,
it's funny when somebody

00:07:33.634 --> 00:07:35.267
drives a car into a restaurant.

00:07:35.267 --> 00:07:37.400
In real life,
people get crushed and killed.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:40.334
-Yeah.
-So we were eating our pancakes,

00:07:40.334 --> 00:07:42.100
and there was a guy
sitting over there.

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:44.968
And I didn't know who it was.
It was Val Kilmer.

00:07:44.968 --> 00:07:49.300
And he was looking at me,
saying obscene things to me.

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:52.033
And I said, "David,
unless that's an actor

00:07:52.033 --> 00:07:54.267
learning his lines
or somebody with Tourette's,

00:07:54.267 --> 00:07:56.467
he's clearly telling me
to go 'F' myself."

00:07:56.467 --> 00:07:57.534
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:57.534 --> 00:07:58.567
-And he looked
over his shoulder.

00:07:58.567 --> 00:08:00.334
He says, "Oh, it's Val Kilmer.

00:08:00.334 --> 00:08:02.000
He's a really nice guy,"
and he invited me over.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:03.167
I said, "Hi.
My name's Matthew."

00:08:03.167 --> 00:08:05.734
"I know who you are.
I'm sick of you, man."

00:08:05.734 --> 00:08:07.400
And I said, like, "What's up?"

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:09.067
And I had been on a run.

00:08:09.067 --> 00:08:10.801
You know, actors,
every once in a while,

00:08:10.801 --> 00:08:13.834
we get on a really good run,
and I'd done a bunch of movies.

00:08:13.834 --> 00:08:15.267
And he said,
"Now you're doing

00:08:15.267 --> 00:08:16.968
Kubrick's movie,
'Full Metal Jacket.'"

00:08:16.968 --> 00:08:19.367
I said, "You don't
have to worry about that.

00:08:19.367 --> 00:08:20.834
I'm not doing
'Full Metal Jacket.'

00:08:20.834 --> 00:08:22.801
If you have a problem
with the rest of my career,

00:08:22.801 --> 00:08:24.934
why don't we go outside
and settle it?"

00:08:24.934 --> 00:08:27.167
'Cause I'm the youngest
of seven kids.

00:08:27.167 --> 00:08:28.667
I've been fighting
my whole life.

00:08:28.667 --> 00:08:31.133
[ Laughter ]
And David stopped it.

00:08:31.133 --> 00:08:33.067
And I ran out,
and I called my agent.

00:08:33.067 --> 00:08:34.534
And I said,
"This actor, Val Kilmer,

00:08:34.534 --> 00:08:36.234
just said
I'm doing Kubrick's movie,"

00:08:36.234 --> 00:08:38.000
and he said, "I don't know
anything about it."

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:39.267
I said, "I don't, either."

00:08:39.267 --> 00:08:41.067
But I know he makes his movies
with Warner Bros.,

00:08:41.067 --> 00:08:42.834
and I'd just done
"Vision Quest."

00:08:42.834 --> 00:08:47.367
And Alan Parker was editing
"Birdy" in London.

00:08:47.367 --> 00:08:49.167
I said, "Let's just
send him some clips."

00:08:49.167 --> 00:08:50.667
And we did,
and then a month later,

00:08:50.667 --> 00:08:53.567
I got the script in the mail --
"Hi. My name is Stanley Kubrick.

00:08:53.567 --> 00:08:55.167
I'm a film director."
[ Laughter ]

00:08:55.167 --> 00:08:57.734
"And I wonder if you'd be
interested in being in my film."

00:08:57.734 --> 00:08:58.934
-Unbelievable!
-Yeah.

00:08:58.934 --> 00:09:01.567
-Val Kilmer called it!
-Val Kilmer called it.

00:09:01.567 --> 00:09:03.767
So I have to say,
"Tank you, Val Kilmer"!

00:09:03.767 --> 00:09:04.934
-Thank you, Val Kilmer.
-Thank you, Val Kilmer.

00:09:04.934 --> 00:09:06.467
-For everything!
-For everything.

00:09:06.467 --> 00:09:07.567
-That's a great story.

00:09:07.567 --> 00:09:09.000
Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:11.467
Next time, whether I'm talking
to myself or not, say hello.

00:09:11.467 --> 00:09:12.801
Matthew Modine, everybody!

00:09:12.801 --> 00:09:16.200
Volume 2 of season 4
of Netflix's "Stranger Things"

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:17.601
begins streaming July 1st.

00:09:17.601 --> 00:09:19.968
We'll be right back
with music from Billy Strings!

